# Mockito 5's default "inline" mock maker rewrites bytecode at
# runtime using Byte Buddy. The Byte Buddy version bundled with
# Mockito 5.14.2 tops out at Java 24 class files (major version
# 68); under JDK 25 (major 69) Byte Buddy's class reader throws
#     "Java 25 (69) is not supported by the current version of
#      Byte Buddy which officially supports Java 24 (68)"
# from inside Mockito.mock(...). jawelte's MockitoMockFactory
# catches the exception and returns null — at which point the
# auto-mock synthetic bean is never registered and the @Inject
# site fails with UnsatisfiedResolutionException at container
# boot.
#
# This is a temporary library-version-lag situation, not a
# permanent JDK restriction. Under JDK 17-24 the inline maker
# worked without any config (that's why earlier jawelte versions
# never shipped this file). Once Mockito ships with a Byte Buddy
# that knows Java 25 class files, this file can be deleted.
#
# Three workarounds for the gap:
#  (a) the file you're reading — select Mockito's subclass mock
#      maker. Byte Buddy generates a real subclass per mocked
#      type and never reads the original bytecode, so the class-
#      file-version check is irrelevant. Mocks every interface
#      and non-final class — sufficient for jawelte's auto-mock
#      layer (final JDK classes are filtered out upstream).
#  (b) launch the JVM with -Dnet.bytebuddy.experimental=true;
#      Byte Buddy then accepts the unknown class-file version.
#      The inline maker stays in use (so final classes can still
#      be mocked) at the cost of carrying a JVM flag.
#  (c) wait for a Mockito release that bundles a Java-25-aware
#      Byte Buddy and drop both (a) and (b).

mock-maker-subclass
